WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.436
Cyber security failures.

00:00:02.386 --> 00:00:04.795
Canada's digital defence gap.

00:00:04.795 --> 00:00:09.193
This is the two hundred and fiftieth investigation page on TENET5.

00:00:09.193 --> 00:00:25.715
It documents Canada's cyber security vulnerabilities: CRA credential stuffing attacks that compromised thousands of accounts, federal government network breaches, critical infrastructure vulnerabilities in energy and telecom sectors, and the documented gap between Canada's cyber threat environment and its defensive capabilities.

00:00:25.715 --> 00:00:31.897
The Canadian Centre for Cyber Security has issued multiple advisories about nation-state cyber threats.

00:00:31.897 --> 00:00:42.522
Yet Canada's cyber defensive capability lags behind allies, critical infrastructure operators face inconsistent security requirements, and government systems continue to experience breaches.

00:00:42.522 --> 00:00:45.727
Three documented cyber security failures.

00:00:45.727 --> 00:00:48.863
The digital defence layer of sovereignty failure.

00:00:48.863 --> 00:00:54.204
Cyber security failures complement the physical sovereignty gaps documented across this site.

00:00:54.204 --> 00:01:04.374
Canada can't patrol its own Arctic, can't sell its own energy to alternatives, can't make its own medicine, and can't defend its own digital infrastructure from nation-state cyber attacks.

00:01:04.374 --> 00:01:11.579
The same institutional capture that degrades military capability and prevents physical sovereignty also prevents digital sovereignty.

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:15.420
Captured regulators don't impose binding cyber standards.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.840
Underfunded agencies can't recruit cyber talent.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:25.715
Government systems built on US cloud infrastructure are vulnerable to both foreign hackers and US legal process.

00:01:25.715 --> 00:01:30.829
The digital and physical sovereignty gaps are two expressions of the same institutional failure.
